l2disco — third-party attribution
====================================================================

This module's own code is MIT (see the repository LICENSE). It additionally
reproduces third-party CAPTURED WIRE DATA (raw LLDP/CDP/ARP/DHCP frame bytes,
not source code) whose copyright notice must be preserved in any copy.
Redistributing this module — alone or as part of zig-libs — must keep this
file with it.

This adds no condition beyond MIT's own: it is the same "preserve the notice"
requirement that already applies to zig-libs' own code, owed to a different
project. No third-party SOURCE CODE is present — only fifteen raw frames
(real LLDP/CDP/ARP/DHCP traffic and dissector test fixtures), reproduced as
byte-literal test fixtures in `src/capture_test.zig`.

## the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump — dissector test-suite capture files

Vendored from https://github.com/the-tcpdump-group/tcpdump, `tests/`
directory, pinned at commit `57ca8734c0c14ad4ab294fe67100dadc4fb6ce31`
(`master` branch, 2026-07-30):

  * `tests/lldp_mudurl.pcap` — a real Ubuntu 14.04 host's LLDPDU (frame 1).
  * `tests/LLDP_and_CDP.pcap` — two real Cisco IOS switches, `S1`/`S2`: their
    CDPv2 frames (frames 1-2) and LLDP frames (frames 3-4).
  * `tests/3560_CDP.pcap` — a real Cisco 3560 switch's CDPv2 frame (frame 1).
  * `tests/lldp-infinite-loop-1.pcap` — the exact bytes that once hung
    tcpdump's own LLDP dissector (frame 1; named upstream for that bug).
  * `tests/lldp_8021_linkagg.pcap` — an org-specific (IEEE 802.1 Link
    Aggregation) TLV fragment (frame 1; the TLV *value* only, its 2-byte
    header stripped here).
  * `tests/lldp_mgmt_addr_tlv_asan.pcap` — the exact bytes that once caused
    an AddressSanitizer-caught heap-buffer-overflow READ in tcpdump's own
    LLDP Management Address TLV decode (frame 1 of 2; the TLV *value* only).
  * `tests/lldp_8023_mtu-oobr.pcap` — the exact bytes that once caused an
    out-of-bounds read in tcpdump's own LLDP IEEE 802.3 Maximum Frame Size
    TLV decode (frame 1; the TLV *value* only).
  * `tests/arp-oobr.pcap` — a real ARP request (frame 1 of 2282) plus one
    fuzzer-mutated frame from the same corpus (frame 164 of 2282,
    `hlen=plen=170` in a 46-octet frame — the out-of-bounds-read shape named
    in the file itself).
  * `tests/dhcp-mud.pcap` — a real Raspberry Pi's DHCPREQUEST and the
    matching server DHCPACK (frames 1-2).
  * `tests/dhcp-option-33.pcap` — a real DHCPOFFER carrying a Static Route
    option (frame 1 of 5).

Excluded from vendoring, deliberately:

  * `tests/lldp-infinite-loop-2.pcap`, `lldp_asan.pcap`,
    `lldp_8023_mtu-oobr.pcap`'s sibling files, and the remaining ~2100
    "normal traffic" frames of `arp-oobr.pcap` / ~2100 fuzzed frames of the
    same corpus, and the remaining frames of `dhcp-option-33.pcap` (4 of 5,
    each just one more Static-Route entry appended to the same base OFFER) —
    each already represented in shape by a sibling frame that IS vendored;
    vendoring every near-duplicate would multiply file size without
    anchoring anything new.
  * `tests/dhcp-rfc3004.pcap`, `dhcp-rfc4388.pcap`, `dhcp-rfc5859.pcap`,
    `dhcp6-*.pcap`, `dhcpv6-*.pcap` and the other DHCP fixtures in the same
    directory — DHCPv6 or option shapes already covered by the two vendored
    DHCPv4 captures; not vendored to keep scope to what is actually anchored.
  * `tests/aarp-*.pcap`, `arcnet-*-arp-*.pcap` — AppleTalk ARP and ARP over
    ARCNET, both outside this module's documented Ethernet+IPv4 `EthIpv4`
    scope (the generic `arp.Packet` handles arbitrary hardware/protocol
    lengths already, and is exercised by the vendored `arp-oobr.pcap` frame).

